Team: Marketplace backend, Tannenwald Retail GmbH. Lint rule set:

L1  SQL must not be built from request-controlled values by string interpolation or concatenation unless the value is (a) passed as a bound parameter or (b) restricted to a fixed allow-list through strict comparison before use.
L2  Controller actions returning lists must paginate.
L3  Authorization via $this->authorize() or a policy middleware is required on every action that reads tenant data.

Only L1 is being evaluated in this review.

Snippet (app/Http/Controllers/ProductSearchController.php):

  public function index(Request $request)
  {
      $this->authorize('viewAny', Product::class);

      $allowedSort = ['name', 'price', 'created_at'];
      $sort = $request->input('sort', 'name');
      if (!in_array($sort, $allowedSort, true)) {
          $sort = 'name';
      }
      $dir = $request->input('dir') === 'desc' ? 'desc' : 'asc';

      $q = trim((string) $request->input('q', ''));

      $query = Product::query()
          ->where('tenant_id', $request->user()->tenant_id)
          ->selectRaw('products.*, COUNT(reviews.id) AS review_count')
          ->leftJoin('reviews', 'reviews.product_id', '=', 'products.id')
          ->groupBy('products.id');

      if ($q !== '') {
          $query->whereRaw('LOWER(products.name) LIKE ?', ['%' . strtolower($q) . '%']);
      }

      $query->orderByRaw("products.{$sort} {$dir}");

      return ProductResource::collection($query->paginate(25));
  }

Note: the string concatenation in the LIKE binding builds the bound value, not the SQL text.

